From 862ce1ef089cbb2f623b49ca060571a568dc4553 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Graham Taylor-Jones <graham.taylor-jones@cargt.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 13:02:47 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] stm32-stgen: don't panic on suspend-time RTC read
 failure

This is the fix for the actual customer-reported bug: poweroff
resets the board (IWDG) instead of powering off. Root cause,
isolated via the checkpoint tracing in earlier commits:
stm32_stgen_pm_suspend() panics when it can't read the RTC
calendar, and on this board that read fails deterministically at
this exact point in the suspend sequence (previous commit made
stm32_rtc_read_calendar() return an error here instead of
panicking itself, surfacing this).

The RTC read in stm32_stgen_pm_suspend() is a best-effort save
used only to compensate the STGEN counter for elapsed time on
resume - not essential to actually suspending or powering off.
Panicking here turns a cosmetic time-keeping inaccuracy into a
full system crash, which is what's producing the IWDG reset
instead of a clean power-off. Log and continue instead: on
failure, stgen_d.calendar simply keeps its last successfully-read
value, and resume's drift compensation is slightly stale rather
than exact.

Signed-off-by: Graham Taylor-Jones <graham.taylor-jones@cargt.com>
---
 core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c b/core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c
index 63a8b07e9..b2771061b 100644
--- a/core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c
+++ b/core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c
@@ -98,8 +98,17 @@
 	 * it spins forever instead of timing out - eventually caught only
 	 * by the independent hardware watchdog resetting the board.
 	 */
+	/*
+	 * Don't let a failure here (e.g. the RTC's "registers
+	 * synchronized" flag not settling in time) panic the whole
+	 * system mid system-off: this is a best-effort save for drift
+	 * compensation on resume, not essential to actually cutting
+	 * power. Worst case on a failed read, stgen_d.calendar keeps
+	 * whatever it was last successfully read as, and resume's drift
+	 * compensation is slightly stale rather than exact.
+	 */
 	if (stm32_rtc_get_calendar(stgen_d.calendar))
-		panic("Could not get RTC calendar at suspend");
+		EMSG("Could not get RTC calendar at suspend, continuing anyway");
 
 	/*
 	 * Disable STGEN counter. At this point, it should be stopped by
--
